An if, elif, else chain Python checks the conditions in order. If score is at least 90 it runs the A branch and skips the rest. Otherwise, if score is at least 75 it runs the B branch. If neither is true, the else branch runs and prints C. Exactly one branch runs. if score &gt;= 90 elif score &gt;= 75 else print("A") print("B") print("C") no no yes yes always checked top to bottom; the first true branch runs, the rest are skipped <pre><code>score = 82
if score &gt;= 90:
    print("A")
elif score &gt;= 75:
    print("B")
else:
    print("C")
</code></pre> <p>This prints <code>B</code>. Python tests each condition in order and runs the block under the first one that is true. Everything after it is skipped, even if it would also be true.</p> <p>That makes the order of the conditions part of the logic. Swap the first two tests and a score of 95 prints <code>B</code>, because <code>95 &gt;= 75</code> is checked first.</p> <p>The block under each line is marked only by indentation: four spaces, no braces. The colon at the end of the <code>if</code> line says "a block follows".</p>
